## What I changed

This is Prefab's "update many aggregates from one event" case, built from two annotated types (no hand-written controller/service/consumer — those are generated).

**`src/main/java/com/example/watch/PriceWatch.java`** — added a multicast event handler:

```java
@EventHandler
@Multicast(queryMethod = "findByProduct", parameters = "product", failWhenEmpty = false)
public PriceWatch onPriceChanged(PriceChanged event) {
    return new PriceWatch(id, version, email, product, event.price());
}
```

- `@Multicast` runs `findByProduct` against the `PriceWatchRepositoryMixin` for **every** watch of `event.product()`, so all watches of that product get their `currentPrice` set to the new price; watches of other products aren't queried, so they're untouched.
- `failWhenEmpty = false` is required because the default is `true` (the generated service would otherwise throw on an empty result, triggering a retry/dead-letter). With `false`, a product with no watches makes the handler return early — the event is consumed successfully with no retry or DLQ.

**`src/main/java/com/example/watch/PriceWatchRepositoryMixin.java`** (new) — the query the handler resolves:

```java
@RepositoryMixin(PriceWatch.class)
public interface PriceWatchRepositoryMixin {
    List<PriceWatch> findByProduct(Reference<Product> product);
}
```

## Verification

- Prefab generated the consumer (`PriceWatchKafkaConsumer` on topic `prices`) and `PriceWatchService.onPriceChanged`, which does `findByProduct(event.product())`, returns early when empty, else maps each matched watch through the handler and saves them.
- `GET /price-watches/{id}` already exposes `currentPrice` via the generated `PriceWatchResponse`.
- I ran the task's acceptance test (`PriceWatchAcceptanceTest`) locally by temporarily adding the test + its `benchmark.support.Api` helper: **1 passed** (both lamp watches track the new price; the desk watch stays at 200). I then removed those temporary files so the project tree contains only the two domain changes.
- `mvn verify` → **BUILD SUCCESS**, conformance tests green.

No changes to `Product`, `PriceChanged`, or `Application` were needed — the `PUT /products/{id}/price` publish already existed.

Done. The project compiles and `mvn verify` passes (including the generated JSON round-trip conformance test).

## What I changed

**New file — `OrderPlaced.java`** (the event):
```java
@Event(topic = "orders", platform = Event.Platform.KAFKA, serialization = Event.Serialization.JSON)
public record OrderPlaced(
        @PartitioningKey Reference<Order> order,
        String customer,
        BigDecimal total
) {}
```

**`Order.java`** — bound a publisher and published in the `@Create` constructor:
```java
private static final EventPublisher<OrderPlaced> PLACED = EventPublisher.bind(OrderPlaced.class);

@Create
public Order(@NotNull String customer, @NotNull BigDecimal total) {
    this(Reference.create(), 0L, customer, total);
    PLACED.publish(new OrderPlaced(id, customer, total));
}
```

## How each requirement is met

- **Publish to Kafka topic `orders`** — `@Event(topic = "orders", platform = Event.Platform.KAFKA)`. Prefab generates an `OrderPlacedProducer` and registers the Kafka event type.
- **Partition by the order** — `@PartitioningKey` on the `order` (`Reference<Order>`) component, so Prefab registers a partition-key extractor and every event of the same order lands on the same partition.
- **JSON payload** — `serialization = Event.Serialization.JSON`.
- **On create** — `PLACED.publish(...)` in the `@Create` constructor fires whenever `POST /orders` creates an `Order`. The event carries `order`, `customer`, and `total` exactly as specified.

The generated `agent-context.md` confirms `OrderPlaced` on topic `orders` with a Kafka producer, and the `OrderPlacedEventConformanceTest` (serialization round-trip) passes. No hand-written controller, producer, migration, or DTO — all wiring is Prefab-generated.

Done. Both reporting queries are on the orders repository that beans already inject, added through Prefab's sanctioned mechanism rather than a second repository.

## What I did

**1. Added a `@RepositoryMixin` interface** — `src/main/java/com/example/ordering/application/OrderRepositoryMixin.java`:

```java
@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)
public interface OrderRepositoryMixin {
    List<Order> findByCustomerEmail(String customerEmail);
    long countByStatus(OrderStatus status);
}
```

These are Spring Data derived queries (`findBy…` / `countBy…` over the `Order` record components `customerEmail` and `status`).

**2. Verified the merge.** Per how the Prefab processor works (`PersistenceWriter#writeRepository` adds each `@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)` interface as a superinterface of the generated repository), the generated `com.example.ordering.application.OrderRepository` now reads:

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends CrudRepository<Order, String>,
        PagingAndSortingRepository<Order, String>, OrderRepositoryMixin { }
```

So `OrderService` and the reporting job — which inject `OrderRepository` — can call `findByCustomerEmail(...)` and `countByStatus(...)` directly, on the one repository Prefab generates. No second repository was created: the mixin is not a Spring Data `Repository` and `@RepositoryMixin(Order.class)` is the path the compiler diagnostic itself recommends, and the `HandWrittenPersistenceCheck` skips it (it returned early, so no "duplicate repository" warning).

**3. Added an integration test** — `src/test/java/com/example/ordering/application/OrderRepositoryMixinQueryTest.java` — `@IntegrationTest` (in-memory, no Docker) that injects the generated `OrderRepository`, saves three orders, and asserts both queries behave correctly.

## Verification

- `mvn -DskipTests compile` — clean, mixin merged into `OrderRepository`.
- `mvn -Dtest=OrderRepositoryMixinQueryTest test` — passes.
- `mvn verify` (full suite, including Prefab's generated wiring/conformance tests) — **BUILD SUCCESS**, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`. No Prefab "hand-written repository" diagnostics.

New files only (no generated sources edited, no `target/` changes): `OrderRepositoryMixin.java` (main) and `OrderRepositoryMixinQueryTest.java` (test).

