## What I did

**Domain type** — `src/main/java/com/example/catalog/Book.java`: a Prefab aggregate (no hand-written controller/service/repository). Validation is placed on the `@Create` constructor parameters, not the record components:

```java
@Aggregate
@GetById
public record Book(
        @Id Reference<Book> id,
        @Version long version,
        String title,
        String author,
        String isbn,
        int publicationYear
) {
    @Create
    public Book(
            @NotBlank @Size(max = 200) String title,
            @NotBlank String author,
            @NotBlank String isbn,
            @NotNull Integer publicationYear
    ) {
        this(Reference.create(), 0L, title, author, isbn, publicationYear);
    }
}
```

Prefab generated the rest at compile time (`target/generated-sources`):
- `BookController` → `POST /books` returns `201 Created` with a `Location` header of `/books/{id}` (ends in the new id); `GET /books/{id}` returns the `BookResponse` JSON or `404`.
- `BookResponse` exposes `id`, `title`, `author`, `isbn`, `publicationYear` (plus `version`).
- `BookService` / `BookRepository` and the `book` table in PostgreSQL via `prefab-postgres`.
- `BookClient` test helper.

**Test** — `src/test/java/com/example/catalog/BookIntegrationTest.java` (`@IntegrationTest`, in-memory, no Docker): asserts `POST /books` returns 201 with a `Location` ending in the new id, that the book reads back with all five fields, and that an unknown id returns 404.

## Verification
- `mvn -DskipTests compile` — model + generated code compiles.
- `mvn -Dtest=BookIntegrationTest test` — 2/2 pass.
- `mvn clean verify` — **BUILD SUCCESS** (exit 0); the only test class runs 2 tests, 0 failures/errors.

The endpoints match the spec exactly: `POST /books` (201 + `Location` ending in the id) and `GET /books/{id}` (JSON with `id`, `title`, `author`, `isbn`, `publicationYear`, or 404), persisted to PostgreSQL.

One environment note (not a code issue): this sandbox's default `java.io.tmpdir` points at the read-only system temp, which makes Surefire log a recoverable fork warning; the build still completes successfully, and `mvn clean verify` returns exit 0 without any workaround.

The task is complete. The full build passes with all 7 tests green.

## What I changed

**`src/main/java/com/example/crm/Customer.java`** — added Jakarta validation on the `@Create` constructor parameters (the cheat sheet's rule: validation goes on the constructor params, not the record components):

```java
@Create
public Customer(@NotBlank @Size(max = 100) String name,
                @NotBlank @Email String email) {
    this(Reference.create(), 0L, name, email);
}
```

Prefab copies these constraints onto the generated `CreateCustomerRequest`, so `POST /customers` rejects invalid bodies with `400` before anything is persisted, and valid bodies still return `201`:
- `name` → `@NotBlank` (required, non-blank) + `@Size(max = 100)` (≤100 chars)
- `email` → `@NotBlank` (required) + `@Email` (valid address)

**`src/test/java/com/example/crm/CustomerValidationIntegrationTest.java`** — a focused `@IntegrationTest` that asserts:
- a valid request is created and stored (`201`, record exists);
- blank/missing `name`, blank/missing `email`, an invalid `email`, and a `name` >100 chars each return `400` and store nothing (verified via a before/after `CustomerRepository.count()`, so the check is order-independent).

## Verification
- `mvn -DskipTests compile` and `mvn test-compile` — clean; generated `CreateCustomerRequest` carries `@NotBlank @Size(max=100)` on `name` and `@NotBlank @Email` on `email`.
- `mvn -Dtest=CustomerValidationIntegrationTest test` — 7/7 pass.
- `mvn verify` — `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 7, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.

No `@RestController`/`@Service`/`@Repository` or migrations were written by hand — the model change flows through the annotations, and Prefab generates the rest.
